 ],
 "literal": "As drums sound and conches roar, taking rest / in the assembly place of the ancient, old banyan tree, / like the truthful words of the Kōcar of Nālūr, / it has become true, O friend! With the select-anklet-wearing, / red-bladed, bright-speared young hero, / the union of the young woman with gathered bangles on her forearm.",
 "translation": "O friend! Like the truthful words of the Kōcar of Nālūr, who appear at the assembly place under the ancient, old banyan tree where drums sound and conches roar as they take rest, the union of this young woman—whose forearms are adorned with gathered bangles—with the young hero who wears select anklets and carries a bright spear with a red blade, has indeed become a reality.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! பறைகள் ஒலிக்க, சங்குகள் முழங்க, பறவைகள் தங்கி ஓய்வெடுக்கும் பழமையான பெரிய ஆலமரத்தின் பொதுமன்றத்தில் கூடிய நாலூர் கோசர்களின் வாய்மை தவறாத சொற்களைப் போல, அழகிய வீரக்கழலையும், சிவந்த இலையடி போன்ற கூர்மையான பிரகாசமான வேலையும் ஏந்திய காளையோடு, திரண்ட வளையல்களை அணிந்த முன்கையையுடைய இப்பெண்ணின் நட்பு உண்மையாகிவிட்டது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the foster-mother to the heroine's companion after the heroine has eloped with her lover. She expresses her relief and acceptance, noting that the couple's union has now become an undeniable, solemnized reality. She compares the truth of their marriage to the infallible, truthful words of the Kōcar clan, who gather for assembly under the ancient banyan tree of Nālūr. The imagery of the banyan tree, where birds rest amidst the sounding of drums and conches, evokes a sense of public witness and sacred authority. By comparing the elopement to this respected assembly's word, the foster-mother acknowledges that the relationship is now legitimate and permanent.",
